To create a safe phishing simulation for testing purposes, follow these steps:

### Objective: Simulate a Phishing Attack Safely

1. **Create a Fake Login Page**
   - **HTML/CSS:** Design a page resembling a legitimate login interface.
   - Include a header with a company logo, a form with username and password fields, a login button, and a message indicating the account is locked.
   - Use realistic placeholders and familiar elements to mimic a real login screen.

2. **Capture User Input**
   - **JavaScript:** Write a script to capture form data when submitted.
   - Store captured data (e.g., username, password) in a secure manner, using dummy data or encryption if necessary.

3. **Send Simulation Email**
   - **Python SMTP:** Use a library like `smtplib` to send an email from a localhost server.
   - Construct an email with a subject and body, including a link to the fake login page.
   - Ensure the email is marked clearly as a test simulation to avoid confusion.

4. **Implementation Steps:**

   ```python
   # Email Sending
   import smtplib

   host = "localhost"
   port = 1025
   sender_email = "test@example.com"
   recipient_email = "testuser@example.com"
   subject = "Account Security Alert: Your Account Has Been Compromised"
   body = """Hi, your account has been compromised. Please reset your password immediately by clicking this link: http://localhost:8000/login """

   with smtplib.SMTP(host, port) as server:
       server.sendmail(sender_email, recipient_email, ("Test Simulation", body))
   ```

5. **Fake Login Page HTML**

   ```html
   <!DOCTYPE html>
   <html>
   <head>
       <title>Login Page</title>
       <style>
           body {
               font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
               display: flex;
               flex-direction: column;
               align-items: center;
               background-color: #f0f0f0;
           }
           .container {
               width: 350px;
               margin: 50px auto;
               padding: 20px;
               border: 1px solid #ccc;
               border-radius: 5px;
           }
           h1 {
               color: #333;
               text-align: center;
           }
           .form-group {
               margin: 10px 0;
           }
           input {
               width: 100%;
               padding: 10px;
               margin-top: 5px;
               border: 1px solid #ccc;
               border-radius: 4px;
           }
           button {
               background-color: #4CAF50;
               color: white;
               padding: 10px 20px;
               border: none;
               border-radius: 4px;
               cursor: pointer;
           }
           button:hover {
               background-color: #45a049;
           }
           .message {
               color: red;
               margin-top: 10px;
               font-size: 0.9em;
           }
       </style>
   </head>
   <body>
       <h1>Account Login</h1>
       <form id="loginForm" action="/submit" method="POST">
           <div class="form-group">
               <input type="text" id="username" name="username" placeholder="Enter your Username" required>
           </div>
           <div class="form-group">
               <input type="password" id="password" name="password" placeholder="Enter your Password" required>
           </div>
           <button type="submit">Login</button>
       </form>
       <div class="message">
           Your account has been temporarily locked. Please reset your password.
       </div>
   </body>
   </html>
   ```

6. **Data Capture and Storage**
   - Use a text file to log captured data.
   - Ensure data is stored securely or cleared after testing.

7. **Testing and Security**
   - Run the simulation on a local network or a test domain.
   - Use dummy credentials and avoid real user data.
   - Clearly mark the simulation as testing to prevent confusion.

8. **Shutdown/Cleanup**
   - Terminate the SMTP server after testing.
   - Remove or secure the fake login page and data after use.

**Note:** Always ensure the simulation is conducted in a controlled environment and is clearly marked to avoid causing panic or confusion. This approach allows for safe testing of user responses without actual harm.<｜end▁of▁sentence｜>

